'''Dunedin''' is an electorate to the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]]. It was created for the {{NZ election link|2020}}.

==Population centres==
The Dunedin electorate consists of the central, north and western suburbs of [[Dunedin]], the [[Otago Peninsula]] communities of [[Harington Point]], [[Otakou]], [[Harwood, New Zealand|Harwood]], [[Portobello, New Zealand|Portobello]], [[Broad Bay, New Zealand|Broad Bay]], [[Company Bay]], [[Pukehiki]], [[Macandrew Bay]], [[Waverley, Dunedin|Waverley]], [[Shiel Hill]] and [[Ocean Grove, New Zealand|Ocean Grove]], and the towns north of the city including [[Port Chalmers]], [[Aramoana, Otago|Aramoana]], [[Pūrākaunui]], [[Waitati]], [[Evansdale, New Zealand|Evansdale]], [[Warrington, New Zealand|Warrington]], [[Seacliff, New Zealand|Seacliff]], [[Karitane]] with its northern boundary north of [[Waikouaiti]]. The urban and suburban core covers the [[Central Dunedin|CBD]], [[Dunedin North]], [[City Rise]], [[North East Valley]], [[Pine Hill, New Zealand|Pine Hill]], [[Leith Valley]], [[Maori Hill]], [[Wakari]], [[Halfway Bush]], [[Brockville, New Zealand|Brockville]], [[Roslyn, Dunedin|Roslyn]], [[Kaikorai Valley]], [[Mornington, Dunedin|Mornington]] and [[Maryhill, New Zealand|Maryhill]].<ref name="parllib">{{cite web| url= https://www.parliament.nz/en/mps-and-electorates/electorate-profiles/dunedin-electorate-profile/ |title=Dunedin – Electorate Profile |publisher= Parliamentary Library |date= 27 April 2021 |access-date= 17 September 2025}}</ref>

==History==
{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Dunedin.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
In the 2019–20 electoral boundary review, all five electorates in the Otago and Southland regions had to be adjusted as they exceeded the 5% population quota. Some electorates were over and some were under the quota, but taken together they were almost exactly on quota. Both {{NZ electorate link|Dunedin North}} and [[Dunedin South]] were significantly below quota and had to gain population. [[Otago Peninsula]] was moved from Dunedin South to Dunedin North; this area has a population of about 8,000 people. A large area from the northern part of the Dunedin North electorate (including Palmerston, Macraes, and Herbert) went to the {{NZ electorate link|Waitaki}} electorate, a loss of 2,500 people.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Houlahan |first1=Mike |url= https://www.odt.co.nz/regions/south-otago/huge-implications-boundary-changes |access-date=27 April 2020 |work=[[Otago Daily Times]] |date=21 November 2019 |title='Huge implications' in boundary changes}}</ref> Adding the southern Dunedin area of Otago Peninsula made it necessary for both Dunedin North and Dunedin South to be recreated under new names, with the former Dunedin North plus Otago Peninsula now called the Dunedin electorate. The former Dunedin South electorate extended into the south Otago area and the {{NZ electorate link|Taieri}} electorate was recreated.<ref>{{cite book | isbn=978-0-473-51728-1 |title=NZ electorate boundary review |url= https://vote.nz/assets/Boundary-Review/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2020.pdf |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=27 April 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Houlahan |first1=Mike |url= https://www.odt.co.nz/news/dunedin/new-electorate-boundaries-names-revealed |access-date=27 April 2020 |work=[[Otago Daily Times]] |date=17 April 2020 |title=New electorate boundaries, names revealed}}</ref>

